From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Zahary Karadjov <zahary@metacraft-labs.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Allow macOS pools, matching what scale sets already accept
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`CreateScaleSetParams.Validate()` does not constrain `OSType` at all, so a
macOS runner scale set is accepted today and works end to end with an
external provider. The pool path does constrain it:

    // runner/types.go
    supportedOSType map[params.OSType]struct{} = map[params.OSType]struct{}{
        params.Linux:   {},
        params.Windows: {},
    }

and `appendTagsToCreatePoolParams()` — the sole caller of
`IsSupportedOSType()` — rejects anything outside that map, so the same
declaration expressed as a pool fails with

    error fetching pool params: invalid OS type macos

There is nothing behind the allow-list that actually requires Linux or
Windows for an external provider. `OSType` on a pool is stored, exported
as the `os_type` metrics label, used for template selection, and handed to
the provider verbatim in `BootstrapInstance` — the provider decides what
that means. JIT config generation and runner registration never look at it.

Add `params.MacOS` to the map, so pools and scale sets agree on which
operating systems a provider may be asked to serve.

Depends on `MacOS` existing in cloudbase/garm-provider-common — see the
companion patch in this directory (provider-common-fix.patch) and PR.md.

Signed-off-by: Zahary Karadjov <zahary@metacraft-labs.com>
---
 runner/types.go | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

diff --git a/runner/types.go b/runner/types.go
index ee41188..73644ef 100644
--- a/runner/types.go
+++ b/runner/types.go
@@ -29,6 +29,7 @@ var (
 	supportedOSType map[params.OSType]struct{} = map[params.OSType]struct{}{
 		params.Linux:   {},
 		params.Windows: {},
+		params.MacOS:   {},
 	}

 	// These are the architectures that Github supports.
--
